<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<ContentView
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
    x:Class="DungeonMaster.App.Controls.DmSearchBar"
    x:Name="Root">

    <!-- BackgroundColor is restated on purpose: DmBorderTavernStyle inherits
         BackgroundColor="Transparent" from DmBorderBase, so a Border with a surface of its own
         has to say so again. The StrokeShape element is kept for the same class of reason — the
         style's inherited shape is DmRadiusDefault (3) and the search field is deliberately on
         DmRadiusLoose (4), matching the cards below it rather than the toolbars. -->
    <Border
        x:Name="SearchBorder"
        Style="{StaticResource DmBorderTavernStyle}"
        BackgroundColor="{StaticResource DmSurfaceTavernRaised}"
        Stroke="{StaticResource DmBorderTavernBrush}"
        Padding="{StaticResource DmPaddingControl}">
        <Border.StrokeShape>
            <RoundRectangle CornerRadius="{StaticResource DmRadiusLoose}" />
        </Border.StrokeShape>
        <!-- MinimumHeightRequest is stated here and nowhere else. A control carrying Style= is
             ignored by the implicit Entry style in Implicit.xaml, which is what supplies the
             touch target; adopting DmEntryTavern therefore opts out of it. That floor is
             load-bearing for THIS field specifically — it was measured dropping from 51px to
             42px when the stock styles were deleted (see Implicit.xaml). The floor is restated
             at the call site rather than on DmEntryBase because putting it on the base would
             grow CreateDungeonPopup's Entry and Editor, which have never had a minimum. What
             the call site names is now DmTouchTargetMinimum rather than a literal 44; the
             reasoning above is unchanged, only the source of the number is. -->
        <Entry
            x:Name="SearchEntry"
            AutomationId="DungeonSearchEntry"
            SemanticProperties.Description="Search dungeons"
            Style="{StaticResource DmEntryTavern}"
            MinimumHeightRequest="{StaticResource DmTouchTargetMinimum}"
            Text="{Binding SearchText, Source={x:Reference Root}, Mode=TwoWay}"
            Placeholder="Search dungeons..." />
        <!-- THE FOCUS APPEARANCE LIVES HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE, and it is declarative so a theme
             dictionary can reach it. It used to be two code-behind assignments to
             SearchBorder.Stroke, which no dictionary could reach at all (UIKIT-013,
             docs/audit/04-app-controls-and-theme.md:545-551).

             WHY A TRIGGER RATHER THAN A VISUAL STATE, because the obvious answer is wrong twice
             over. Focused/Unfocused are raised by the ENTRY, but the element that shows focus is
             this outer BORDER, so a CommonStates group on the Border never fires — that
             mismatch is exactly what the old code-behind was working around. MAUI's answer to
             it is a CommonStates group on the Entry whose Setters carry
             TargetName="SearchBorder" and Property="Border.Stroke" (the "set state on multiple
             elements" form). That was built and it works, but the fully qualified property name
             it REQUIRES is unreadable to
             TokenTypeMatchesTargetPropertyTests.EveryDmTokenBindingTargetsAPropertyOfAKnownType,
             which attributes a Setter to its nearest element ancestor and so sees
             "Entry.Border.Stroke" — an element+property pair no table can name. Teaching that
             fact about TargetName means editing DungeonMaster.Tests, which this control's task
             does not own.

             So the dependency is inverted instead: the Border reads the Entry's IsFocused
             rather than the Entry pushing at the Border. Same single owner for the focus
             appearance, same two tokens, and the Setter's target is an ordinary Border.Stroke.
             The resting value is the Stroke attribute above; DataTrigger outranks a local value
             (see the precedence note in Controls.Base.xaml) and restores it on deactivation, so
             the two states are stated as a pair the way DmCard.xaml states Normal/PointerOver.

             The brushes are the border-brush tokens themselves now, not
             DmTheme.FloorBorderActive/FloorBorderNormal, whose names describe the floor card.
             Nothing about the rendered colour changes: those two members resolve these very
             keys (DmTheme.cs:91-99). -->
        <Border.Triggers>
            <DataTrigger TargetType="Border"
                Binding="{Binding IsFocused, Source={x:Reference SearchEntry}}"
                Value="True">
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource DmBorderTavernHoverBrush}" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Border.Triggers>
    </Border>
</ContentView>
